      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"colette-le-ble-en-herbe-aa5c7bfe3b6367b5\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":2,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.89,\"analysis\":\"Vinca’s “Je t’aime!” is primarily a desperate avowal presenting her love as true. Its description as part of an emotional eruption and as a cry analogous to “Adieu!” gives it some independently expressive force, though it remains intelligible chiefly as an intensely delivered avowal. The surrounding refusal of separation lends weak commitment-like resonance, but the core words do not clearly undertake a relational obligation.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is whether the farewell comparison establishes genuinely expressive/reflexive force or merely characterizes the intensity and valedictory circumstances of a truth-conditional avowal. Performative force is only weakly suggested by the adjacent claim that she can no longer leave him.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"reported\",\"description\":\"The narrator retrospectively reports Vinca’s earlier direct utterance while reproducing its exact words in quotation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Vinca utters the words amid tears, desperate confessions, and horror at an impending separation. The simile “comme on crie ‘Adieu!’” frames the declaration as a despairing, almost valedictory cry: she avows love precisely when their future appears unreachable and separation intolerable. Her accompanying “Je ne peux plus te quitter!” intensifies the attachment and provides limited commitment-like resonance, while the narration’s account of their lifelong bond supports the avowal’s sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“elle avait éclaté en larmes, en aveux désespérés”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration belongs to a sequence of desperate confessions, strongly supporting truth-conditional avowal and providing some evidence of expressive eruption.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Elle avait crié: «Je t’aime!» comme on crie «Adieu!»”\",\"supports\":\"The exact utterance explicitly avows love; comparison to a farewell cry gives positive, though not decisive, evidence for expressive/reflexive force beyond mere emphatic delivery.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“«Je ne peux plus te quitter!» avec des yeux pleins d’horreur”\",\"supports\":\"The adjacent utterance situates the avowal within intolerable separation and supplies weak commitment-like or relational-undertaking resonance.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“L’amour, grandi avant eux, avait enchanté leur enfance” and Philippe “la chérissait”\",\"supports\":\"The narration presents their love as a longstanding relationship rather than an instrumental, rote, or semantically empty use of the formula.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the central avowal, E captures the explicitly framed quality of a despairing emotional cry, and a small P score records limited commitment resonance. The valedictory and tragic framing is contextual nuance rather than a failure of the compact ontology.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
